---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/main.rs
expression: command.clone().render_long_help().to_string()
---
Export spec graph to stdout in various formats

Usage: specforge export [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - graph:    every entity with its fields, file and line (Graph Protocol)
          - context:  every entity's fields, annotations, methods and verify, its headline on top, without file and line, for an agent
          - brief:    id, kind and title
          - dot:      Graphviz
          - markdown: a document a reviewer reads: each entity's statements, fields, links and obligations
          
          [default: graph]

      --scope <SCOPE>
          Scope export to subgraph reachable from this entity ID

      --depth <DEPTH>
          With --scope, how many hops from the scoped entity to include

      --kinds <KINDS>
          Keep only entities of these kinds (comma-separated, or the flag repeated); the scoped entity always stays

      --no-schema
          Suppress schema embedding in `graph` exports (keeps format_version 1.0)

      --with-schema
          Embed the schema in `context` and `brief` exports, and in a `graph` export under --max-tokens. They leave it out by default: an agent reading the export needs the entities, and the schema is most of the bytes. Under --max-tokens an embedded schema counts toward the budget, and a budget it doesn't fit in fails (E062)

      --schema-version <SCHEMA_VERSION>
          Fail unless a reader of this schema version reads the export: the same major version, not newer than the project's schema

      --max-tokens <MAX_TOKENS>
          Token budget for the export: keeps the most central entities that fit. The schema is left out unless --with-schema is given, and then counts toward the budget. The export lists the dropped entities under `token_budget`; below one entity it is the envelope with no entities, and below even that it fails (E062). Ignored by `dot` and `markdown`

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the graph format's JSON answer, one per line, e.g. '.nodes[kind=phase].id'. The path is steps: `.key`, `[n]` (digits, no leading zero), `[]` (every item) and `[<key>=<value>]` (the items whose field <key> is <value>, e.g. `[kind=plan]`: <key> may be dotted, `[fields.result=issue]`, and a number, boolean or null is compared as JSON, `[fields.backlog=true]`, `[fields.wave=2]`; an object or array is never selected); a `.` before `[` may be left out, and `.` alone is the whole answer. One line per value reached: a string bare (a newline in it is printed as is), an integer as is and any other number as an f64 (`1.0`, `-0.0`, `1e3` as `1000.0`), a boolean, object or array as compact JSON; null or a missing value prints nothing, and is not an error. It takes --format graph or json.

      --fields <KEYS>
          With --pick: print one tab-separated row per item the path reaches, one cell per comma-separated key, in order, e.g. --pick '.nodes[kind=commit]' --fields fields.hash,fields.subject. A key may be dotted. A cell prints as --pick prints a value, with a missing value or null as an empty cell, so the rows never misalign; a backslash, tab, newline or carriage return in a cell prints as \\, \t, \n or \r.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
